# End-of-line policy.
#
# The checked-out bytes must not depend on who cloned. `0.3.0a10` was built from
# a tree where 42 shipped modules carried CRLF and the rest LF, because nothing
# here declared `.py` and the bytes therefore followed the cloner's
# `core.autocrlf` -- true for a Windows clone, unset for a Linux one. Every file
# was byte-for-byte the same program, and the installed-content digest still
# named a tree no checkout can reproduce. Identity is asserted on installed
# content, so the content must be a property of the commit and not of the
# machine that read it.
#
# Later lines win, so this file reads top to bottom as: a baseline, then the
# exceptions, then the things that must never be touched at all.

# --------------------------------------------------------------- 1. baseline
#
# Everything git detects as text reaches the disk with LF. Declared as a
# baseline rather than a list because a list only covers the extensions someone
# remembered: `.cu`, `.mod`, `.sum`, `.tpl`, `Dockerfile`, `LICENSE`, `NOTICE`
# and the extensionless files are all text this repository maintains, and any
# one of them left undeclared makes a Windows clone dirty on arrival and
# unbuildable under the dirty-tree guard.
* text=auto eol=lf

# ------------------------------------------------- 2. maintained source types
#
# Redundant with the baseline and stated anyway. If the baseline is ever
# narrowed, the intent for these types should survive the edit rather than
# quietly change with it.
*.py    text eol=lf
*.sh    text eol=lf
*.c     text eol=lf
*.h     text eol=lf
*.cpp   text eol=lf
*.cu    text eol=lf
*.go    text eol=lf
*.js    text eol=lf
*.html  text eol=lf
*.css   text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
*.jsonl text eol=lf
*.toml  text eol=lf
*.yaml  text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf
*.txt   text eol=lf

# `.sh` above is load-bearing. A shell script with CRLF fails at the shebang:
# the kernel takes the trailing carriage return as part of the interpreter path
# and reports "bad interpreter: /usr/bin/env bash^M: no such file or directory".
# Every repro script in `scripts/repro/` is run directly on Linux.

# ------------------------------------------------------- 3. Windows scripts
#
# Windows batch must reach the disk with CRLF. An LF-only `.cmd` made batch
# label resolution fail intermittently once already -- `rm` broke while `models`
# worked. That used to work by accident because Windows clones default to
# core.autocrlf=true; it is stated here so it does not depend on that.
#
# `.ps1` is grouped with them deliberately. PowerShell tolerates either ending,
# but these are Windows-side scripts (`install.ps1`, `agents/windows/*.ps1`) and
# an undeclared type is exactly the gap this whole file exists to close.
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# --------------------------------------------------------------- 4. binaries
#
# Declared explicitly rather than left to the `text=auto` heuristic. The
# heuristic is good and it is still a guess, and `.whl` files here are release
# artifacts whose hashes are published.
*.docx  binary
*.pdf   binary
*.png   binary
*.pptx  binary
*.whl   binary
*.gz    binary
*.tar   binary
*.zip   binary
*.exe   binary
*.so    binary
*.bin   binary
*.safetensors binary

# ---------------------------------------------------- 5. frozen evidence, last
#
# `-text` means no end-of-line conversion in either direction: a checkout
# reproduces the exact bytes that were committed, whatever `core.autocrlf` says.
#
# This is not tidiness. `release/` holds frozen evidence whose SHA-256 values are
# published in release records and tag messages, and `SHA256SUMS.frozen` files
# that verify them. Normalising those bytes would change hashes that other
# documents assert, which is the one thing this repository never does. Last in
# the file so it overrides every rule above.
release/**      -text
docs/evidence/** -text
